sdom justice  temperance and courage much of that has  to do with all that realm of things that  in themselves don't really matter but  how we comport ourselves how we use them  what we do with those things does in  fact make a difference and so Cicero is  going to have Cato at several points  make some very interesting observations  the first of these is in Section 12  where he says that it is indeed prop  the word there is propria it's  translated here in this text as  essential it's on the essence of virtue  to exercise some sort of choice choice  where among the things that are in  accordance with nature  now virtue itself is in accordance with  nature right and virtuous actions are in  accordance with nature our human nature  fully developed but there's an entire  realm of indifference that are still in  accordance with nature they just don't  have a positive moral value they have a  value in other respects and virtue makes  choices among them it ranks them in  relation to each other it chooses them  and rejects the things that are not in  accordance with nature or if you have to  choose between multiple ones which ones  do you actually select if you have to  choose say between health and wealth or  choose between the job that you really  enjoy but doesn't make that much money  and the job that you don't really enjoy  that much but isn't honor us and you  might make more money in that these are  the sorts of things where virtue makes  selections and he goes on and he says  philosophers who make all things  absolutely equal rendering them  indistinguishable either as better or  worse leaving no room for selection and  the word there select selection is  select Sione right leaving no room for  that to be used to be employed have  abolished virtue itself there's  something that has to do with the nature  of virtue that it extends out into the  world of indifference and makes choices  about them this is even more the case if  we think about one virtue in particular